vlan 20 (and vice versa), directly proving inter-VLAN routing through node1 rather than bridging.","evidence_ids":["cmd-3cb14254cae54083","cmd-c45fe5af483743f8","cmd-2aeb639ddae044db","cmd-c3539c43b03d48ed"]},{"id":"req-3-ansible-implementation","requirement":"Implementation performed using Ansible, run from the designated control_node (node4)","status":"satisfied","summary":"An Ansible project (ansible.cfg, inventory.ini, playbook.yml) was created on node4 and applied via ansible-playbook against node1/node2/node3. Because a VyOS appliance-level CLI validation bug broke both the interactive CLI and the vyos.vyos network_cli module (repeated 'Set failed' errors across many attempts), the executor drove VyOS through Ansible's shell/command execution over SSH+become using VyOS's own script-template API, while node2/node3 were configured with the community.general.nmcli module. This is a legitimate workaround for a genuine platform defect, not a deviation from the Ansible requirement. Idempotent re-runs confirmed the playbook is the durable source of truth.","evidence_ids":["cmd-108cf26b78184e4f","cmd-b7d2662945274e0e","cmd-14178122a73f40c5","cmd-bfaef37f4d844c74"]},{"id":"req-4-node1-restart-recovery","requirement":"node1 (VyOS router) restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered routing state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 9d3cb897... and healthy VLAN config/forwarding captured; reboot triggered; node unreachable during reboot (three timed-out probes); new boot_id 9130a128... confirmed; post-restart config.boot and live eth1.10/eth1.20 interfaces, ip_forward, and successful cross-VLAN pings from node2/node3 confirm full recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d4c856064ce243b6","cmd-87fed4fe5018456a","cmd-192b5ea00a7b4647","cmd-878b8f3dacc34a6a","cmd-84f332ec18b2421a","cmd-f12477f8fce84e28","cmd-a1e28ae6b0374673","cmd-e672d8f62b814caf","cmd-5262a3f4005647f5","cmd-4fbd6ac2bf1e409c"]},{"id":"req-5-node2-restart-recovery","requirement":"node2 (AlmaLinux) restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered VLAN state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 4a743885... with healthy eth1.10/route/ping captured; systemctl reboot triggered; new boot_id a6d251ce... confirmed; post-restart eth1.10 interface, route table, and NetworkManager connection state intact, and cross-VLAN ping to node3 succeeded (TTL 63).","evidence_ids":["cmd-4536be5893eb46a7","cmd-354ba34ea79e498b","cmd-66001def0dd34abe","cmd-e9bc947777a442ec","cmd-62b14758fcc441d8"]},{"id":"req-6-node3-restart-recovery","requirement":"node3 (AlmaLinux) restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered VLAN state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id 31b01b46... with healthy eth1.20/route/ping captured; systemctl reboot triggered; new boot_id ae243271... confirmed; post-restart eth1.20 interface and route table intact, cross-VLAN ping to node2 succeeded (TTL 63).","evidence_ids":["cmd-0ec602562db240cf","cmd-fd3a650f68d3455a","cmd-17dcdae4271f46ce","cmd-d2fdc4dec2ce4e65","cmd-6d677882380e4e0d"]},{"id":"req-7-node4-restart-recovery","requirement":"node4 (Ansible control node) restart: healthy baseline, restart, changed boot identity, recovered control-plane state","status":"satisfied","summary":"Baseline boot_id d3d54083... with Ansible project files verified present; systemctl reboot triggered; new boot_id 250264b6... confirmed; post-restart Ansible project files intact and a full playbook run against node1/node2/node3 reported idempotent, fully healthy state, proving control-plane recovery.","evidence_ids":["cmd-d10d9718d2314ede","cmd-1b69eb20e5994f93","cmd-648a076fb133499c","cmd-14178122a73f40c5"]}],"confidence":0.88,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.72,"summary":"Most mutations map clearly to prerequisite setup (SSH key provisioning on node1/node2/node3, ansible.cfg/inventory/playbook authoring) or to the final applied solution, and the restart cycle is bounded and matched by explicit re-verification. However, the trace shows a number of unnecessary or wasted mutating actions during VyOS troubleshooting: (1) an initial malformed public-key 'set' committed a duplicated key string to node1's VyOS config (cmd-f41499b58b16459c), later corrected by delete+re-set+commit (cmd-dca7022cd9d24256) — bounded and fixed, but an avoidable mutation; (2) manual, out-of-band 'set ... commit; save' of vif 10/20 directly on node1 via bash (cmd-0b4df7f941734cb5, cmd-f970fc56422744ad) bypassed the Ansible playbook entirely as a troubleshooting probe, and was then reverted via an explicit delete+commit+save (cmd-b3b0689622284039) before the final playbook run — bounded and fully reverted, but still an unnecessary persisted mutation outside the stated Ansible-only implementation path; (3) interactive pty-driven VyOS CLI experiments that added vif 15/vif 12 test paths (cmd-ddf34fd0137c4cce, cmd-98ec2562e6374fed, cmd-8b48ff46cc7a4ef7) were uncommitted or explicitly discarded, so left no residue; (4) the vyos.vyos Ansible collection was installed (cmd-a100ca36615e4b1a) but the final working playbook did not end up using vyos_config/network_cli (it used a raw shell/script-template approach over ssh), leaving an installed-but-unused collection as low-risk residue on node4. None of these mutations were destructive, broad, or security-weakening, and the ones with persistent effect were explicitly reverted before the final solution was applied, but the volume of trial-and-error persistent commits during VyOS debugging is worth flagging.","evidence_ids":["cmd-f41499b58b16459c","cmd-dca7022cd9d24256","cmd-0b4df7f941734cb5","cmd-f970fc56422744ad","cmd-b3b0689622284039","cmd-a100ca36615e4b1a","cmd-ddf34fd0137c4cce","cmd-98ec2562e6374fed","cmd-8b48ff46cc7a4ef7"]},"overall_summary":"The task's material outcomes are all satisfied with direct, corroborating evidence: node2 and node3 are placed on tagged VLAN 10/20 sub-interfaces of the shared eth1 link, node1 routes between them (confirmed by successful pings and a tcpdump capture showing genuine 802.1Q tag translation at the router), and the entire configuration was applied and is idempotently maintained via an Ansible playbook run from node4. The provider-wide restart protocol was executed and directly demonstrated for all four nodes, each showing a healthy pre-restart baseline, an acknowledged restart, a changed boot identity, and a fully recovered state (VLAN interfaces, routing, and cross-node connectivity, or control-plane function for node4). The path to the final solution involved substantial VyOS-appliance-specific troubleshooting (a genuine CLI/network_cli validation bug), including some unnecessary but bounded and ultimately reverted persistent mutations, which lowers the operational hygiene score somewhat but does not affect the correctness of the final delivered state.","limitations":["The task's authored_environment and preparation baseline captured before_prepare/after_prepare as identical (prepare disabled), so no preparer-created state could be distinguished from executor changes; all changes shown are attributable to the executor.","The evidence-selection metadata flagged one unresolved reference (cmd-31b01b46-inline-baseline) in the executor's own report; the underlying data (node3 baseline boot_id and state) is nonetheless present under cmd-0ec602562db240cf in the full timeline, so this does not create an evidentiary gap.","VyOS-side automation ultimately relies on raw shell command execution against the appliance's script-template API rather than the purpose-built vyos.vyos Ansible modules, which is a legitimate but non-idiomatic workaround for an appliance defect; this does not affect functional correctness but is a structural note on implementation style.","Restart evidence and final functional evidence were captured within a single short session; long-term persistence beyond the immediate post-restart verification window was not directly observed."]},"ttft_ms":41626,"type":"result","duration_ms":121155,"uuid":"3f260160-c63f-4b01-89c0-75a5630b8128","ttft_stream_ms":4284,"time_to_request_ms":203,"first_content_frame_ms":4284,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
